1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,144
The saved school outline lists a 60-second tourniquet competency,
burns, heat and cold, altitude syndromes, submersion, crush, complex
trauma and a rapid load-and-go decision.

2
00:00:12,144 --> 00:00:23,280
The stopwatch values are station benchmarks. They do not replace
correct indication, safety, effective treatment, team communication or
clinical judgment.

3
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:35,184
Use the school’s own equipment and rubric under instructor
supervision. This lesson prepares your reasoning; it does not certify
a procedure or reproduce an assessed checkoff answer.

4
00:00:35,184 --> 00:00:57,336
The live school lab summary specifies application, cessation of
bleeding and time-marking in under sixty seconds. Keep that less-than
boundary for the station, rather than silently changing it to sixty
seconds or less. This is an integrated supervised skill target, not
permission to omit assessment or an effective bleeding check.

5
00:00:57,336 --> 00:01:12,360
Current BCEHS PR03 applies to severe extremity bleeding when the
indicated prior method has not controlled it; it requires complete
bleeding control and documentation of application time. Follow the
actual local procedure.

6
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:25,920
During supervised practice, use the school’s specified trainer and
technique. The instructor should confirm placement, securement,
windlass/locking method, reassessment and documentation.

7
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:36,624
A fast incomplete application is not a pass. Confirm the expected
station rubric, then practise until the effective sequence is both
accurate and repeatable.

8
00:01:36,624 --> 00:02:06,360
For supervised practice with the approved windlass trainer, identify
uncontrolled external limb bleeding and follow the current procedure’s
direct-pressure attempt. If ineffective, place the device on skin,
five to eight centimetres above the injury or high on the limb,
avoiding a joint. Tighten the strap, then the windlass until bleeding
stops; secure the windlass. The instructor checks technique and actual
effect, not just completion of movements.

9
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:32,904
Record the actual application time and procedure, reassess bleeding
and the patient, and communicate the intervention. PR03 calls for
consultation if management or transport may extend beyond two hours;
that is not an instruction to loosen or remove the device at a timer.
In class, practise correct placement and repeated assessment on the
trainer, using the school’s device and checkoff requirements.

10
00:02:32,904 --> 00:02:46,320
In this original practice case, a sixty-one-year-old is brought from a
detached workshop fire. The patient has a hoarse voice, cough and soot
around the lips, with a visible partial-thickness forearm injury.

11
00:02:46,320 --> 00:03:01,752
Treat the changing voice and soot as airway-risk information. Continue
ABC assessment, consider combustion exposure and follow current H10,
B01 and J02 guidance within the authorized provider role.

12
00:03:01,752 --> 00:03:15,480
Use the Rule of Nines or a suitable hand method to communicate an
estimate of partial/deeper burn area. Superficial erythema is excluded
from BCEHS TBSA, and the estimate is not exact.

13
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:33,096
The school outline calls CO-oximetry mandatory in every enclosed fire.
Current BCEHS J02 supports oxygen and consultation for suspected CO,
while available CO-oximetry is an adjunct; do not teach the school
phrase as a universal prerequisite.

14
00:03:33,096 --> 00:04:00,312
Use the adult Rule of Nines as an approximate model: head and neck
nine percent, each complete arm nine, each complete leg eighteen,
front trunk eighteen, back trunk eighteen, and perineum one. The
linked BCEHS chart also offers a more detailed subunit method with
different allocations. Choose and state one suitable method, rather
than mixing its numbers with nines or applying adult proportions to a
child.

15
00:04:00,312 --> 00:04:31,608
Depth changes what you count. Superficial injury is limited to the
epidermis, often red and dry. Partial-thickness injury extends into
dermis; superficial partial injury commonly blisters and weeps, while
deeper injury may be patchy or less sensitive. Full-thickness injury
destroys dermis and may look leathery or charred with reduced
sensation. Appearance evolves: record findings and uncertainty rather
than diagnosing depth from pain alone.

16
00:04:31,608 --> 00:05:02,328
For an explicitly fictional update to the fire case, the whole left
arm now has partial-thickness findings, while front-trunk redness is
superficial only. Using adult nines, count nine percent, not nine plus
eighteen. This is a teaching update, not a fact from the original
patient description. Report the method, depth and estimate; ongoing
hoarseness and soot still make airway and toxic exposure urgent, and
the number does not prescribe a fluid dose.

17
00:05:02,328 --> 00:05:16,824
A twenty-eight-year-old climber at three thousand three hundred metres
has a worsening headache and now cannot walk a straight line. There is
no resting dyspnea reported. This is an original practice case, not
the saved school case.

18
00:05:16,824 --> 00:05:31,080
New ataxia or altered mental status raises concern for high-altitude
cerebral edema. Ask about ascent and sleep altitude, assess neurologic
and respiratory status, and look for alternative causes.

19
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:42,024
Resting dyspnea, cough or worsening oxygenation would raise concern
for HAPE, but its absence does not make HACE safe. Avoid further
ascent and reduce exertion.

20
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:55,536
Urgent descent is central for severe altitude illness. If oxygen is
available and indicated, start it while organizing safe descent;
neither action should be delayed solely to complete the other.

21
00:05:55,536 --> 00:06:09,888
Compare a person who feels faint and sweaty after prolonged exertion
with one who is confused and hot. Altered mental status with heat
exposure raises a high-consequence concern and needs the current
hyperthermia pathway.

22
00:06:09,888 --> 00:06:22,728
Current BCEHS I02 calls for prompt cooling and continuation during
transport when possible. Choose a method that remains safe for the
patient’s mobility, airway, trauma and the crew.

23
00:06:22,728 --> 00:06:36,144
For cold exposure, remove the person from further cooling, dry and
insulate, and handle gently. Peripheral thermometer readings are
imperfect; mental status and physiologic trends matter.

24
00:06:36,144 --> 00:06:47,544
The cold-water submersion station can involve both hypoxia and
hypothermia. Continue to reassess breathing and perfusion instead of
treating temperature as the only problem.

25
00:06:47,544 --> 00:07:00,528
A rescuer must not enter unsafe water without the required training,
flotation and rescue plan. Use appropriate rescue resources and
maintain scene safety before patient contact.

26
00:07:00,528 --> 00:07:13,944
After removal, assess breathing, oxygenation, airway contamination,
perfusion, temperature and mechanism-related trauma. Drowning may
follow a medical event rather than cause it.

27
00:07:13,944 --> 00:07:27,888
Current H12 directs care through the appropriate resuscitation pathway
with particular attention to airway and oxygenation. Cold-water cases
can require specialist consultation and continued resuscitation
decisions.

28
00:07:27,888 --> 00:07:39,864
Fresh versus salt water does not create two field treatment recipes. A
seemingly minor rescue may still have later respiratory risk, so
follow current conveyance guidance.

29
00:07:39,864 --> 00:07:51,312
A mechanic is trapped under a collapsed vehicle lift in a cold garage.
The compression duration is uncertain; the lower leg is swollen and
the patient is pale and anxious.

30
00:07:51,312 --> 00:08:07,200
The team verifies machinery and structural stability, obtains rescue
resources, identifies access/egress and reports the entrapment time as
uncertain. The release is a coordinated operation, not a spontaneous
pull.

31
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:19,464
Use the current H02 pathway and required consultation to plan what is
feasible before release. Anticipate systemic deterioration and prepare
monitoring and reassessment.

32
00:08:19,464 --> 00:08:31,536
The case does not supply a universal fluid amount or automatic
tourniquet rule. Mechanism, duration, muscle burden, hemodynamics and
provider authorization shape the plan.

33
00:08:31,536 --> 00:08:46,032
Before moving, decide whether the patient can assist and whether a
lift, carry, slide or evacuation device is appropriate. Check the
route for stairs, turns, weather, contaminated surfaces and obstacles.

34
00:08:46,032 --> 00:08:58,176
Assign a clear leader and one coordinated count. Protect airway
access, oxygen, bleeding control, temperature measures and monitoring
from being unintentionally disconnected.

35
00:08:58,176 --> 00:09:08,640
Reassess immediately after packaging or movement. A new breathing
problem, loss of pulse quality, increased pain or displaced device
changes the plan.

36
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:20,928
The saved outline asks learners to make a rapid thirty-second load-
and-go decision. Treat this as a school verbalization exercise to
surface priorities, not an operational stopwatch law.

37
00:09:20,928 --> 00:09:34,536
State the threat, the reason transport cannot wait, the minimum safe
preparation and who will continue care during movement. Identify any
rescue or extrication step that must happen first.

38
00:09:34,536 --> 00:09:46,248
A rapid decision is not a rushed move. Package safely, maintain airway
and hemorrhage priorities, secure equipment and reassess after each
major change.

39
00:09:46,248 --> 00:10:14,856
This fire-exposed patient has hoarseness, soot and increasing effort.
Airway deterioration and combustion toxicity are immediate concerns. I
recommend rapid conveyance with indicated oxygen and early higher-
level support. Before moving, confirm a safe route, secure the patient
and equipment, and assign airway observation. Reassess after movement,
and send a pre-alert with actual care and response.

40
00:10:14,856 --> 00:10:42,456
That is an original worked decision, not a school answer or a new
diagnosis. It names the threat, choice, essential preparation and
continuing care. Say it in your own words and time the rehearsal
against the school’s thirty-second target. If breathing is inadequate,
indicated ventilation cannot wait for completion of a speech or
loading. Revise the plan when new findings or rescue conditions
change.

41
00:10:42,456 --> 00:10:54,456
After each station, say what the task was testing, what you observed,
what action you took and what changed. A score without a reasoned
debrief can hide an unsafe shortcut.

42
00:10:54,456 --> 00:11:06,624
Ask the instructor to clarify the official sequence, device model,
time target and documentation requirement. Do not infer an unknown
school rubric from a generic textbook.

43
00:11:06,624 --> 00:11:17,448
Repeat the station until the skill is both safe and reproducible.
Narrated preparation supports practice but cannot replace witnessed
competency sign-off.
